  isp_base: "2d6x10 plus 10 per level of experience"
  powers: ["Mind Block", "Sense Evil", "See Aura", "Empathy"]
  powers_starting_groups:
    - { count: 3, categories: ["Sensitive"], note: "Three sensitive powers." }
    - { count: 3, categories: ["Physical"], note: "Three physical powers." }
    - { count: 3, categories: ["Healing"], note: "Three healing powers." }
  categories_allowed: ["Sensitive", "Physical", "Healing"]
natural_abilities:
  - { name: "Shapeshifting", description: "Any humanoid form, though the pleasurer''s weight never changes. Hair, fur and scales can be imitated. More than that, a pleasurer reads the images in a person''s mind and takes the shape of what it finds there - loved ones, private fantasies, famous people. The book states the disguise ability that comes with it as a percentage, and it is granted as a skill: 70% plus 2% per level of experience." }
  - { name: "Tele-Empathy and Empathic Dependence", description: "Senses feelings and surface thoughts, costing no I.S.P., at a range of 100 feet (30.5 m) plus 30 feet (9.1 m) per level of experience. The pleasurer also feels what one or more other people feel as though the feelings were its own; a few can invert the charge and feel the opposite, turning someone else''s pain into their own pleasure. A performer can tune into a crowd and take a surge off it. The power is shut off at will by raising a mind block - EXCEPT when the emotions around it are extreme or a crowd has lost itself in the moment, where there is an 01-60% chance the pleasurer cannot let go." }
  - { name: "Horror Factor", description: "Varies with the shape taken; typically no more than 12. A number the pleasurer projects, which the sheet has no field for." }
  - { name: "M.D.C.", description: "By armour only. A pleasurer is an S.D.C. creature and carries no mega-damage of its own." }
  - { name: "Size and Weight", description: "Size varies with the current shape. Weight is 130 to 200 lbs (59 to 91 kg) and does NOT change when the shape does." }
  - { name: "Average Life Span", description: "200 years." }
side_effects: "EMPATHY IS THE ADDICTION. A pleasurer can become addicted to one person''s emotional state or to the frenzy of a crowd, and most above seventh level have developed 1d4 phobias or obsessions - the G.M. or player may pick other insanities where they fit better, though these are beings of very high mental endurance. The book files this under Vulnerabilities/Penalties; nothing here is computed."
skills:
  occ_skills:
    - { choose: 4, from: ["Language: Other"], bonus: 20, note: "Four Languages of Choice (+20%). The catalog''s repeatable Language: Other row is what an any-language pick resolves through." }
    - { name: "Dance", bonus: 20, note: "+20% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Play Musical Instrument", bonus: 10, note: "Printed as Play Musical Instrument (Two of Choice) (+10%). The catalog holds ONE Play Musical Instrument row rather than one per instrument, so the two instruments the book grants are one skill here." }
    - { name: "Sing", bonus: 20, note: "+20% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "First Aid", bonus: 5, note: "+5% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Acrobatics", base: 0, per_level: 0, note: "Granted with no bonus of its own." }
    - { name: "T.V./Video", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { choose: 1, categories: ["Weapon Proficiencies"], note: "W.P.: One of Choice." }
    - { name: "Hand to Hand: Basic", base: 0, per_level: 0, note: "May be changed to Expert at the cost of one other skill, or to Martial Arts - Assassin if of an evil alignment - at the cost of two." }
    - { name: "Disguise", base: 70, per_level: 2, note: "The book prints this under Natural Abilities rather than in the skill list: shapeshifting gives a disguise ability equal to 70% +2% per level of experience. Granted as the catalog''s Disguise row at exactly those numbers." }
  occ_related_skills:
    count: 8
    categories:
      - { name: "Communications", bonus: 10 }
      - { name: "Domestic", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Electrical", only: ["Basic Electronics"] }
      - { name: "Espionage", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Medical", only: ["Paramedic"] }
      - "Physical"
      - "Pilot"
      - "Pilot Related"
      - { name: "Rogue", bonus: 6 }
      - { name: "Science", only: ["Mathematics: Basic", "Mathematics: Advanced"], bonus: 10 }
      - { name: "Technical", bonus: 10 }
      - "Weapon Proficiencies"
    schedule: [{ level: 3, count: 2 }, { level: 6, count: 2 }, { level: 9, count: 1 }, { level: 12, count: 1 }]
    note: "Eight at creation, two more at levels three and six, one more at levels nine and twelve; every new skill starts at level one proficiency. Mechanical, Military and Wilderness are printed None and are omitted rather than listed - see the extraction notes."
  secondary_skills:
    count: 5
    note: "Five secondary skills from the same list, at the base skill level and without the bonuses in parentheses. The book repeats that the any/only/none limits above still apply."
extraction_notes: |
  - Read from the OCR cache of a scan and verified against a 200 dpi render of
    printed 89, which carries the whole stat block, both natural abilities, the
    psionics line, the R.C.C. skill list and the related and secondary lists.
    The lore starts low on printed 88 under the heading "Pleasurer R.C.C." and
    the entry closes on printed 89 at Secondary Skills; printed 90 opens a new
    section, "Other Worlds", so nothing runs on. The survey''s playable table
    gave the range as 88-90 and the real range is 88-89.
  - THE BOOK PRINTS "P.B. N/A" AND NO P.B. DIE IS INVENTED. A pleasurer wears
    whatever face its client wants, so beauty is not a number it has. The
    attribute is simply absent from `attribute_dice`, the same treatment the
    Norse Giant already gets for a book that lists seven attributes. Writing a
    fixed value there would be worse than absent: `rollAttribute` in js/dice.js
    parses only NdM forms and falls back to 3d6 on anything else, rewriting the
    notation as it goes. See BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT.md F8.
  - S.D.C. IS A POOL BONUS, NOT `sdc_base`. The book prints "1D6x10 + 40 S.D.C.
    plus skill and O.C.C. bonuses", which is the cumulative shape: written as
    `sdc_base` it would REPLACE the occupation''s roll instead of adding to it,
    because `combineClasses` gives the race''s pool precedence. Stored as
    `bonuses.pools.sdc`. A race with no occupation then has no base at all - a
    pool bonus cannot conjure a pool into existence - so the entry
    `pleasurer: ''1D6''` was added to CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS in js/compose.js, which
    the smoke test requires of any class stating neither `sdc_base` nor
    `mdc_base`. 1D6 because a race is never a man of arms.
  - Hit points are "Normal", so no `hit_points_base` is stated and the core rule
    supplies it.
  - THE DISGUISE PERCENTAGE IS A SKILL AND IS GRANTED AS ONE. The book states it
    inside the Shapeshifting ability - 70% +2% per level - and the catalog holds
    a Disguise row, so `base: 70, per_level: 2` says exactly what the book says.
    This is the opposite case from F7: an exact mapping, not a near one.
  - A CATEGORY PRINTED `None` IS AN EXCLUSION. Mechanical, Military and
    Wilderness are printed None and are omitted from `categories` entirely.
    Listing them without a bonus would offer skills the book bars, and the OCR
    renders "Mechanical: None" identically to a category printed with no bonus
    at all.
  - ROGUE IS PRINTED +6%, NOT +5%. Read off the 200 dpi render and then again
    at 400 dpi, and it is a six. THIS IS THE BOOK''S THIRD +6% AND NOT A ONE-OFF:
    a grep of the whole cache finds "Rogue: Any (+6%)" on printed 40 (Galactic
    Tracer), printed 64 (Noro Mystic Warrior) and printed 89, and both of those
    classes already store 6. The book also prints one +8% and one +12%, both on
    printed 42. The Galactic Tracer shipped with a note calling +6% unique to
    it; that sentence is corrected in this batch.
  - A RACE CARRIES NO xp_table. Printed 183 heads the column "Pleasurer &
    Termite Engineers"; composition is race-primary, so a table here would win
    over the occupation''s and silently drop it. The ladder is: 0, 2,151, 4,301,
    8,601, 17,201, 25,501, 36,001, 52,001, 73,001, 98,001, 134,001, 184,001,
    240,001, 295,001, 365,001. Read off a 250 dpi render of printed 183; every
    band''s low is the previous band''s high plus one, which is fifteen readings
    checking each other.
  - THE PSIONIC ALLOWANCE IS THREE FROM EACH OF THREE CATEGORIES, NOT NINE FROM
    THREE. Stored as `powers_starting_groups`, one group per category, which is
    what js/leveling.js reads: a single `powers_starting: 9` would let a player
    take nine sensitive powers and no healing. Four powers are granted outright
    on top - mind block, sense evil, see aura and empathy - and the book gives
    that empathy three times the normal range, which the catalog row cannot
    express and which is recorded in the Tele-Empathy ability instead.
  - NO MONEY, NO EQUIPMENT AND NO CYBERNETICS. The entry states none of the
    three; they come from the O.C.C. if one is taken. Confirmed against the
    render as well as the OCR, because printed 88 and 89 carry the tail of the
    Paradise Federation''s own section above the class.
  - Related and secondary skills come from the O.C.C. in the general case, but
    this race prints its own lists of both, so both are stored.
---

## Lore

Pleasurers are an empathic shape-shifting race with no known homeworld. They are
probably related to the Palladium World''s changelings - which would explain the
number of changelings living on the Paradise worlds - and the other standing
theory is that somebody bio-engineered them, the Gene-Splicers being the usual
suspects. What they are known for is a single pairing of powers: they sense a
person''s deepest desire, and then they become it.

The empathy runs both ways, and that is the important half. A pleasurer feels
what its clients feel, so it takes its own pleasure from giving pleasure. They
are the finest entertainers in the Three Galaxies and a large part of what the
Paradise Federation sells.

## Not a benevolent race

A pleasurer''s idea of a good time is whatever the client is feeling most
strongly, and some of those feelings are lethal - the rush of an overdose, the
last seconds of a fall. Many are addicted to the work and encourage their
patrons deeper into their vices for the sake of the emotions coming off them.
The same hunger can tip into frenzy: a complete loss of self-control, a riot
started for the feeling of it. The ones who feed on pain become torturers and
assassins. A good many work as spies, for the Paradise Foundation and for
whoever else pays.

Available as a player character with the Game Master''s approval. Alignment: any,
but often selfish.

## GM Notes

The empathic dependence is the hook and the leash both. A pleasurer in a crowd
that has lost itself - a concert, a fight, a riot - has an 01-60% chance of not
being able to raise a mind block at all, and is carried along with everyone
else. Above seventh level, expect 1d4 phobias or obsessions to have set in.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'pleasurer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'pleasurer';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-pleasurer-class.sql');
